{"id":"services-networking-cluster-ip-allocation","corpus":"k8s","title":"Service ClusterIP allocation","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/cluster-ip-allocation/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Service ClusterIP allocation"},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, Services are an abstract way to expose an application running on a set of Pods. Services can have a cluster-scoped virtual IP address (using a Service of type: ClusterIP). Clients can connect using that virtual IP address, and Kubernetes then load-balances traffic to that Service across the different backing Pods."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How Service ClusterIPs are allocated?"},{"kind":"p","text":"When Kubernetes needs to assign a virtual IP address for a Service, that assignment happens one of two ways:"},{"kind":"p","text":"dynamically"},{"kind":"p","text":"the cluster's control plane automatically picks a free IP address from within the configured IP range for type: ClusterIP Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"statically"},{"kind":"p","text":"you specify an IP address of your choice, from within the configured IP range for Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Across your whole cluster, every Service ClusterIP must be unique. Trying to create a Service with a specific ClusterIP that has already been allocated will return an error."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Why do you need to reserve Service Cluster IPs?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Sometimes you may want to have Services running in well-known IP addresses, so other components and users in the cluster can use them."},{"kind":"p","text":"The best example is the DNS Service for the cluster. As a soft convention, some Kubernetes installers assign the 10th IP address from the Service IP range to the DNS service. Assuming you configured your cluster with Service IP range 10.96.0.0/16 and you want your DNS Service IP to be 10.96.0.10, you'd have to create a Service like this:"},{"kind":"p","text":"But, as it was explained before, the IP address 10.96.0.10 has not been reserved. If other Services are created before or in parallel with dynamic allocation, there is a chance they can allocate this IP. Hence, you will not be able to create the DNS Service because it will fail with a conflict error."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How can you avoid Service ClusterIP conflicts?"},{"kind":"p","text":"The allocation strategy implemented in Kubernetes to allocate ClusterIPs to Services reduces the risk of collision."},{"kind":"p","text":"The ClusterIP range is divided, based on the formula min(max(16, cidrSize / 16), 256), described as never less than 16 or more than 256 with a graduated step between them."},{"kind":"p","text":"Dynamic IP assignment uses the upper band by default, once this has been exhausted it will use the lower range. This will allow users to use static allocations on the lower band with a low risk of collision."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Examples"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Example 1"},{"kind":"p","text":"This example uses the IP address range: 10.96.0.0/24 (CIDR notation) for the IP addresses of Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Range Size: 28 - 2 = 254Band Offset: min(max(16, 256/16), 256) = min(16, 256) = 16Static band start: 10.96.0.1Static band end: 10.96.0.16Range end: 10.96.0.254"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Example 2"},{"kind":"p","text":"This example uses the IP address range: 10.96.0.0/20 (CIDR notation) for the IP addresses of Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Range Size: 212 - 2 = 4094Band Offset: min(max(16, 4096/16), 256) = min(256, 256) = 256Static band start: 10.96.0.1Static band end: 10.96.1.0Range end: 10.96.15.254"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Example 3"},{"kind":"p","text":"This example uses the IP address range: 10.96.0.0/16 (CIDR notation) for the IP addresses of Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Range Size: 216 - 2 = 65534Band Offset: min(max(16, 65536/16), 256) = min(4096, 256) = 256Static band start: 10.96.0.1Static band ends: 10.96.1.0Range end: 10.96.255.254"},{"kind":"h2","text":"What's next"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about Service External Traffic Policy"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about Connecting Applications with Services"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about Services"}],"lemmas":{"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"services":["service","services"],"abstracts":["abstract","abstracts"],"abstracting":["abstracting","abstract"],"abstracted":["abstracted","abstract"],"exposes":["expose","exposes"],"exposed":["exposed","expose"],"exposing":["exposing","expose"],"running":["running","run"],"runnings":["running","runnings"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"sets":["set","sets"],"setting":["setting","set"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"scopeds":["scopeds","scoped"],"virtuals":["virtuals","virtual"],"addressing":["addressing","address"],"addressed":["addressed","address"],"using":["using","use"],"usings":["using","usings"],"used":["used","use"],"types":["type","types"],"typed":["typed","type"],"typing":["typing","type"],"clients":["client","clients"],"connects":["connects","connect"],"connected":["connected","connect"],"loads":["load","loads"],"loaded":["loaded","load"],"loading":["loading","load"],"balances":["balance","balances"],"balanced":["balanced","balance"],"balancing":["balancing","balance"],"acrosses":["acrosses","across"],"differents":["differents","different"],"backing":["backing","back"],"backings":["backing","backings"],"backs":["back","backs"],"backed":["backed","back"],"allocated":["allocated","allocate"],"allocateds":["allocateds","allocated"],"allocates":["allocates","allocate"],"whens":["whens","when"],"needs":["need","needs"],"needed":["needed","need"],"needing":["needing","need"],"assigns":["assigns","assign"],"assigned":["assigned","assi